        "text": "Gender inequality in the worst of its forms is seen in violence against women folk, Women across the world face violence in various forms, such as physical, psychological and economic violence. It is believed that two third of victims of intimate partner violence are womens According to WHO, one in every five women in European Region has been a violence victim In Pakistani society, women face violence and prejudice in the shape of mental, psychological physical and emotional abuse/ According to an estimate, around 70% to 90% of Pakistani women suffer domestic violence! A survey conducted on 1000 women in Punjab revealed that at least two women are burned every day in domestic violence occurrence Certain heinous social practices like honour killing, marriage with the Quran, Karokari and Swara have become a matter of daily practice in the remote rural areas of Pakistany วกาวา -570 continues to plague many societies all over the world, especially the developing regions As regards the provision of and access to healthcare system, gender disparity such as South Asia, West Asia and China. Regrettably, women all over Pakistan, to health related issues. Pakistan cuts a very sorry figure in terms of the maternal particularly in the remote rural areas, face an extremely awkward situation with respect mortality rate when compared with other countries in the region. It is for various adequate cultural, economic and administrative factors. Due to the non-availabilty of health facilities and qualified female staff, a large number of women lose their lives or their infants during child birth. Generally, a woman is closely tied to the izzat(honour) of the man due to which the free and unattended mobility of women is strictly restricted. This makes it harder for women to receive healthcare services and resources when they need it direly. Resultantly, the health condition of women is much poor in comparison with men.\n\nGender discrimination against women, in terms of education, economic participation and health/ survival opportunities, render them a weak pedestal in the corridors of power. That's why we see that a negligible number of women hold political and institutional decision making positions. A very limited number of them find a place in the parliament and the cabinet of any state. A very few women can be seen as heads of the states, worldwide. The judiciary remains primarily male dominated, especially at higher levels. Same is the case with armed forces, where women's presence in higher ranks is too scanty. As for its relation to religion, Islam enjoins to promote gender equality in all walks of life, be it education, healthcare or economy etc. Gender equality is a part of its jurisprudence and fundamental teachings: \"O mankind! Fear your Lord who (initiated) your creation from a single soul, then from it created its mate, and from these two spread (the creation of) countless men and women.\" (Al-Nisa: 1)\n\nThis verse clearly expounds that men and women are created from a single entity and as a gender, one is not superior to the other.\n\nIslam provides equal rights of taking part in economic activities and property ownership to women.",
