   "content": "<p>Truck Art</p><p>[Summary — the full essay by Rachel Redford is a modern copyrighted text; read it in the printed textbook, pp. 33–35.]</p><p>Across Pakistan — especially in Quetta, Dera Ghazi Khan, Peshawar, Mansehra, Rawalpindi and Karachi — skilled men and boys work in a busy truck-painting trade, covering huge trucks with bright folk art. These decorated trucks have become a tourism symbol recognized around the world.</p><p>Today&#x27;s truckers follow earlier traders who carried goods by camel caravan along similar routes to Central Asia. Just as decorated camels once showed the traders&#x27; origins, truck art today shows where a truck comes from — Peshawar trucks, for example, carry cricket stars, Bab-e-Khyber, mountains and Pashto verse, while Rawalpindi trucks show jets, missiles and famous portraits.</p><p>Truck art is a big business: Karachi alone is said to employ over 50,000 people in family paint workshops. Like a medieval guild, each workshop has specialists — electricians, metal workers, carpenters, upholsterers, a master painter and even a poet for the inscriptions. The work takes six to ten weeks, and most truckers redecorate every three or four years.</p><p>Decoration is expensive — owners often spend more on their trucks than on their houses, sometimes two years’ profits — yet drivers think it worthwhile: it shows thanks for prosperity, attracts customers, keeps up with rivals, and even, as one driver says, makes the police less likely to stop a well-painted truck.</p><p>The designs echo Mughal court decoration and mix East and West, sacred and secular: film stars, cricketers and military heroes appear beside Greek myths, landscapes and calligraphy. Some owners dress their trucks like brides, with hinna patterns, parandas and bell chains, and add charms such as huge eyes and black ribbons against the evil eye.</p><p>In 2002 a Karachi truck painter and a bodywork expert decorated a truck at the Smithsonian Folklife Festival in Washington, D.C., giving Americans a first taste of Pakistani truck art.</p>",
