   "content": "<p>Reading Comprehension</p><p>1. Why did Jahangir Khan go against his doctors&#x27; advice of refraining from taking part in any physical activity?</p><p>2. Read paragraph no 6 of the text and say what did Jahangir Khan do to maintain his physical fitness?</p><p>3. Give a brief detail of Jahangir Khan&#x27;s achievements in the light of the text, &quot; Jahangir Khan – the conqueror&quot;.</p><p>4. Keeping in view the achievements of Jahangir Khan, compare any other national hero in sports with him.</p><p>5. An idiom is the expression whose meaning cannot be inferred from the meanings of the words that make it up. Find out three idiomatic expressions from the text and explain their meanings.</p><p>6. What inspiration do you get from the life of Jahangir Khan?</p><p>Writing Suggestions</p><p>Write an essay on the present state of Squash in Pakistan.</p><p>Oral Communication</p><p>Divide the students into groups on the basis of the choice of their favourite game. Ask each member to share his reasons in the group for liking the particular game in the group.</p><p>Vocabulary and Grammar</p><p>Transitional Devices</p><p>Transitional devices are like bridges between parts of your paper. They are cues that help the reader to interpret ideas a paper develops. Transitional devices are words or phrases that help carry a thought from one sentence to another, from one idea to another, or from one paragraph to another. And finally, transitional devices link sentences and paragraphs together smoothly so that there are no abrupt jumps or breaks between ideas. There are several types of transitional devices, and each category leads readers to make certain connections or assumptions. Some lead readers forward and imply the building of an idea or thought, while others make readers compare ideas or draw conclusions from the preceding thoughts.</p>",
