   "content": "<p>The twins warn Ralph that the tribe hates him and will spread out in a line across the island to hunt him tomorrow; Sam says Roger “is a terror”. Ralph says he will hide in a thicket close by; Sam gives him a piece of meat and, asked what they will do when they catch him, reveals that Roger has sharpened a stick at both ends.</p><p>Ralph hears the twins being punished for talking to him, then sleeps in the ferns. In the morning the tribe rolls rocks into his thicket and Jack sets the forest on fire to smoke him out. Ralph bursts out, fights past the hunters and flees through the burning forest to the beach, where he collapses.</p><p>He looks up to see a naval officer, who has come ashore after seeing the smoke. The officer asks if there are grown-ups, notices the silent painted boys, jokes about “fun and games” and asks if anyone was killed; Ralph says “only two”. (continued on p. 221)</p>",
